  L('m1-1', 'm1', 'Classical AI: search methods and recursion',
`**Classical (symbolic) AI** solves problems by manipulating explicit symbols and rules written by humans. Many problems are framed as **search**: a start state, allowed moves (operators), and a goal test.

• **Breadth-first search** explores level by level: finds the shortest path but uses a lot of memory.
• **Depth-first search** goes deep first: little memory, but may wander down long or infinite paths.
• **Heuristic search** (e.g. A*) uses an estimate of the distance to the goal to expand promising states first.
• **Game-tree search** (minimax, alpha-beta pruning) powered chess programs such as Deep Blue.

**Recursion** means a procedure calls itself on a smaller version of the same problem until it reaches a base case (Towers of Hanoi, tree traversal). Search algorithms are naturally recursive: "explore this node = explore each of its children".`,
['Problem = states + operators + goal test', 'BFS: shortest path, memory hungry; DFS: memory light, can get lost', 'Heuristics guide search; minimax for games', 'Recursion = self-call on a smaller problem + base case']);
  Q('m1-1', 'A route-planning tool must always return the path with the fewest steps and memory is not a constraint. Which uninformed search method fits best?',
