    'A heuristic estimates the remaining cost to the goal. A* combines the cost so far with this estimate to focus the search.');

  L('m1-2', 'm1', 'Limitations of classical AI',
`Symbolic AI worked well in narrow, well-defined domains (chess, logic puzzles, early expert systems) but struggled in the messy real world.

• **Combinatorial explosion**: the number of states grows exponentially, so exhaustive search becomes impossible.
• **Knowledge acquisition bottleneck**: experts must hand-code rules; this is slow, expensive and incomplete.
• **Brittleness**: rule-based systems fail badly on situations the designers did not foresee.
• **Common sense and perception**: recognising faces, speech or handwriting is hard to write down as rules.
• **Uncertainty**: crisp logic handles probabilistic, noisy data poorly.

These limits contributed to the "AI winters" and to the shift towards **machine learning**, where the system learns patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules.`,
['Combinatorial explosion', 'Knowledge acquisition bottleneck', 'Brittle outside designed scope', 'Weak at perception, common sense and uncertainty', 'Led to the data-driven ML paradigm']);
  Q('m1-2', 'A bank built a rule-based expert system for loan approvals in the 1990s. Each new product required months of interviews with credit officers to update the rules. This illustrates which limitation of classical AI?',
