  L('m2c1-1', 'm2c1', 'Machine learning versus classical econometrics',
`**Econometrics** starts from theory: specify a (usually linear) model, estimate parameters, test hypotheses, interpret coefficients and causal effects. Emphasis on **inference** and **explanation**.

**Machine learning** starts from data: flexible, often non-linear models chosen for **predictive accuracy** on unseen data. Emphasis on **prediction**, out-of-sample performance, cross-validation and tuning.

Other differences:
• ML handles very many features and big, unstructured data (text, images).
• ML models are often less interpretable.
• Econometrics relies on distributional assumptions; ML uses regularisation and validation sets to control overfitting.

They overlap: linear and logistic regression appear in both.`,
['Econometrics: theory, inference, coefficients', 'ML: data-driven, prediction, out-of-sample', 'ML: many features, non-linear, less interpretable']);
  Q('m2c1-1', 'Which goal is MORE typical of machine learning than of classical econometrics?',
