  L('m2c1-4', 'm2c1', 'Encoding categorical variables',
`Most algorithms need numbers, so categories must be encoded.

• **Label / ordinal encoding**: map categories to integers (AAA=1, AA=2…). Fine for **ordinal** data; for nominal data it creates a fake order.
• **One-hot encoding**: one binary (dummy) column per category. Standard for **nominal** data. With an intercept in a regression, drop one category to avoid the **dummy variable trap** (perfect multicollinearity).
• **Target / mean encoding**: replace a category by the average target value; compact for high-cardinality variables but risks leakage, so compute it on training data only.
• Too many categories (high cardinality) can explode the number of columns: group rare categories or use target encoding/embeddings.`,
['Ordinal → integers; nominal → one-hot', 'Dummy trap: drop one category with an intercept', 'High cardinality: group, target-encode or embed']);
  Q('m2c1-4', 'A regression with an intercept includes one-hot dummies for all four regions. What problem arises?',
