  L('m2c1-7', 'm2c1', 'Data scaling and transformation',
`Many algorithms (KNN, K-means, SVM, neural networks, PCA, regularised regression) are sensitive to feature scale.

• **Standardisation (z-score)**: z = (x − mean) / standard deviation. Result has mean 0 and standard deviation 1.
• **Min-max normalisation**: x' = (x − min) / (max − min). Result lies in [0, 1]; sensitive to outliers.
• **Log transformation**: compresses right-skewed variables (income, firm size); needs positive values.
• **Box-Cox / Yeo-Johnson**: family of power transformations to make data more normal.

Example: income 60,000 with mean 50,000 and sd 20,000 → z = 0.5. With min 10,000 and max 210,000 → min-max = 50,000/200,000 = 0.25.

Tree-based models are largely insensitive to monotonic scaling.`,
['z = (x − μ)/σ', 'min-max: (x − min)/(max − min)', 'Log for right skew', 'Distance-based methods need scaling; trees do not']);
