  L('m2c1-8', 'm2c1', 'Principal components analysis (PCA)',
`**PCA** reduces the number of features by creating new, **uncorrelated** variables (principal components) that are linear combinations of the originals.

• The 1st component points in the direction of **maximum variance**; each next component captures the most remaining variance and is orthogonal to the previous ones.
• Mathematically: eigenvectors of the covariance (or correlation) matrix; eigenvalues = variance explained.
• Keep the first k components that explain e.g. 90–95% of variance (or use a scree plot "elbow").
• **Standardise first**, otherwise large-scale variables dominate.

Finance classic: yield curve moves are mostly explained by three components: **level, slope and curvature**.

Downsides: components are hard to interpret, PCA is linear and unsupervised (ignores the target).`,
['Uncorrelated linear combinations ordered by variance', 'Eigenvectors/eigenvalues of covariance matrix', 'Standardise first; choose k by variance explained', 'Yield curve: level, slope, curvature']);
